1
00:00:00,103 --> 00:00:01,229
Method.

2
00:00:01,684 --> 00:00:03,362
Sample or record.

3
00:00:03,816 --> 00:00:05,434
Dating evidence.

4
00:00:05,895 --> 00:00:07,664
Dendrochronology.

5
00:00:08,118 --> 00:00:09,928
Wood with growth rings.

6
00:00:10,382 --> 00:00:12,362
Matching ring patterns.

7
00:00:12,816 --> 00:00:14,401
Radiocarbon.

8
00:00:14,855 --> 00:00:17,132
Wood, seeds, bone.

9
00:00:17,592 --> 00:00:22,586
Choose a method by considering the surviving material and the event that started its clock,

10
00:00:22,776 --> 00:00:26,400
rather than selecting a technique by name alone.

11
00:00:27,203 --> 00:00:31,342
After each half-life, half of the parent isotope remains.

12
00:00:31,797 --> 00:00:34,804
Each half-life halves the amount remaining again.

13
00:00:35,258 --> 00:00:40,639
This is an exponential process, unlike a linear decline that removes a fixed amount each

14
00:00:40,653 --> 00:00:42,052
time.

15
00:00:42,855 --> 00:00:44,073
Sample.

16
00:00:44,528 --> 00:00:49,719
Examine carbon in suitable biological material, such as wood or seeds.

17
00:00:50,173 --> 00:00:51,449
Measurement.

18
00:00:51,897 --> 00:00:57,239
Use radiocarbon decay, while considering contamination and carbon sources.

19
00:00:57,699 --> 00:00:59,298
Interpretation.

20
00:00:59,752 --> 00:01:04,147
Calibrate the radiocarbon age and interpret a calendar-age range.

21
00:01:04,607 --> 00:01:09,416
A radiocarbon age is not a calendar date read directly from the material.

22
00:01:09,870 --> 00:01:14,912
Calibration and the sample's history both matter to its interpretation.

23
00:01:15,715 --> 00:01:17,517
K–Ar / Ar–Ar.

24
00:01:17,530 --> 00:01:20,236
Suitable minerals in volcanic rocks.

25
00:01:20,691 --> 00:01:24,151
Consider clock closure and retention during cooling.

26
00:01:24,612 --> 00:01:29,151
Dating ash requires suitable minerals and depositional context.

27
00:01:29,612 --> 00:01:30,572
U–Th.

28
00:01:30,586 --> 00:01:33,915
Suitable carbonates, including speleothems.

29
00:01:34,375 --> 00:01:37,336
Use isotope changes after formation.

30
00:01:37,796 --> 00:01:41,119
Assess initial components and later exchange.

31
00:01:41,573 --> 00:01:46,882
For geological materials, identify the mineral and the event being dated.

32
00:01:47,336 --> 00:01:52,404
Different measurements can constrain different stages of a material's history.

33
00:01:53,207 --> 00:01:54,971
Luminescence dating.

34
00:01:55,433 --> 00:01:57,966
Measure stored signals in minerals.

35
00:01:58,420 --> 00:02:02,815
OSL uses light resetting; TL commonly uses heating.

36
00:02:03,275 --> 00:02:06,821
Assess resetting and the burial dose-rate environment.

37
00:02:07,275 --> 00:02:09,393
Archaeomagnetic dating.

38
00:02:09,854 --> 00:02:13,440
Examine remanent magnetism in fired materials.

39
00:02:13,893 --> 00:02:17,861
Compare cooling-related magnetism with a regional reference.

40
00:02:18,315 --> 00:02:21,696
Check movement after firing and the site location.

41
00:02:22,157 --> 00:02:27,380
Methods involving light, heat or magnetism record different processes.

42
00:02:27,841 --> 00:02:32,344
Check which event is constrained and whether the material is suitable.

43
00:02:33,147 --> 00:02:37,595
Match patterns of wide and narrow rings to assign calendar years.

44
00:02:38,057 --> 00:02:43,452
Dendrochronology matches sequences of variation and tests their connection to known calendar

45
00:02:43,465 --> 00:02:44,478
years.

46
00:02:44,939 --> 00:02:48,380
It involves more than counting visible rings.
